findings.description:
The design says the `arch` gate extends "the no-active-slice / pre-slice-plan path" and sets `pending-review`/`review-failed` (lines 33, 57, 222). It also says it "follow[s] the identical shape" as the code example (line 168). The existing code does not support that shape:
- `deriveSliceStatus()` returns `{status:'no-active-slice'}` immediately when `fileSlice` is empty (`WorkflowNavigator.ts:453-455`). It has no knowledge of arch existence or the slice plan; `parseSlicePlanSafe` runs after it in `getStatus` (`:68-71`). If status became `pending-review` here, the `no-active-slice` guard at `:98` would be skipped and execution would fall into the active-slice path. That path then builds a rationale with `slice.index === null` and `slice: ''`.
- The pre-slice-plan recommendations are produced in `getNext()`, not by status derivation. They come from `detectFirstRunContext` FR-3b and FR-4 (`:413-441`), which return *before* any gate branch, and from `:135-155`.
- The active-slice-with-no-plan case is also a pre-slice-plan position, and the design never mentions it. That case is the `complete-no-plan` guard at `:271-295`, and `needs-design` with no plan behaves the same way.
- `detectDocuments(path, sliceIndex, 'arch')` matches `${sliceIndex}-review.arch.` (`documentDetector.ts:91-96`). An arch review is keyed by the **arch** index (fixture `100-review.arch.only-pass.md`). The design never says which index to pass; it would have to be `extractSliceIndex(project.fileArch)`.

The slice plan assigns this boundary to 241 (line 19, and line 27 folds 244 in). The design needs to state:
- where the arch gate is evaluated: inside `getNext`, before `detectFirstRunContext`, or in `getStatus`;
- which status field carries it when there is no active slice;
- which index is looked up;
- how it combines with the first-run guards and the `complete-no-plan` guard.
